Oh. come on!

251
00:20:12.598 --> 00:20:15.133
What's this then?

252
00:20:15.168 --> 00:20:17.668
There's no problem.
I've got a pass.

253
00:20:17.702 --> 00:20:20.704
But still. why don't I escort
gwen out the back. sir?

254
00:20:20.739 --> 00:20:22.873
<i>- M.P.: You're going back to your billet.
- Good night. gramps.</i>

255
00:20:22.907 --> 00:20:26.076
<i>- Don't wait up.
- M.P.: Show me your pass.</i>

256
00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:29.345
Come on. let's go.

257
00:20:30.881 --> 00:20:33.182
Come on. mo.

258
00:20:38.756 --> 00:20:41.591
Saddle up. pardner. - Stealing an m.
P.'s jeep? Are you kidding me?

259
00:20:41.626 --> 00:20:44.026
- ( starts engine - come on. mo.  Hop in.
- ( starts engine - come on. mo.  Hop in.

260
00:20:46.196 --> 00:20:48.097
You can't do this shit.
john. Not now.

261
00:20:48.131 --> 00:20:49.966
Remember what chesty told you.

262
00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.335
Don't screw this up.

263
00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:07.247
Left.

264
00:21:07.282 --> 00:21:08.749
March.

265
00:21:10.553 --> 00:21:14.122
<i>Line halt.</i>

266
00:21:20.996 --> 00:21:23.464
Detail halt.

267
00:21:23.498 --> 00:21:26.767
Sir. detail is formed.

268
00:21:30.572 --> 00:21:34.408
For extraordinary heroism
and conspicuous gallantry.

269
00:21:34.442 --> 00:21:36.610
Sergeant john basilone.

270
00:21:36.645 --> 00:21:39.146
<i>While at great risk to
his own life.</i>

271
00:21:39.181 --> 00:21:41.649
<i>Fought valiantly to check a savage</i>

272
00:21:41.683 --> 00:21:43.817
<i>And determined japanese assault</i>

273
00:21:43.852 --> 00:21:47.053
While serving the 1st
battalion. 7th marines

274
00:21:47.088 --> 00:21:51.825
In guadalcanal on
October 24 and 25. 1942.

275
00:21:51.859 --> 00:21:55.128
<i>Sergeant john basilone
is hereby awarded</i>

276
00:21:55.163 --> 00:21:56.797
<i>The medal of honor.</i>

277
00:22:15.814 --> 00:22:18.815
<i>Left as we go.</i>

278
00:22:18.849 --> 00:22:22.385
Right face.

279
00:22:24.388 --> 00:22:27.357
Company right hut.

280
00:22:39.996 --> 00:22:42.164
Face right.

281
00:24:02.891 --> 00:24:05.026
So what's your name then?

282
00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:06.960
Bob.

283
00:24:06.995 --> 00:24:08.828
Sobered up. are you. bob?

284
00:24:08.863 --> 00:24:11.031
I'm no fool.

285
00:24:12.433 --> 00:24:13.800
These are for you.

286
00:24:13.834 --> 00:24:17.337
I hope you're hungry.

287
00:24:22.508 --> 00:24:25.143
<i>Man on radio:...Created
by the announcement</i>

288
00:24:25.177 --> 00:24:27.411
<i>That president roosevelt and
winston churchill</i>

289
00:24:27.445 --> 00:24:30.781
<i>Have met at casablanca
and completed...</i>

290
00:24:30.815 --> 00:24:33.483
<i>- Stella: This is bob.
- Leckie.</i>

291
00:24:33.517 --> 00:24:35.050
Bob leckie.

292
00:24:35.085 --> 00:24:37.019
Shake hands with my father baba.

293
00:24:37.053 --> 00:24:39.788
what a grip.

294
00:24:39.822 --> 00:24:42.257
An american grip. eh?

295
00:24:42.291 --> 00:24:44.059
This is my mama.

296
00:24:44.093 --> 00:24:46.694
This is a yank.

297
00:24:46.729 --> 00:24:50.664
Skin and bones.
Like a skeleton. Like a rail.

298
00:24:57.972 --> 00:25:00.206
Stella. his jacket. Ella.

299
00:25:04.378 --> 00:25:06.612
I thought we were
going for a stroll.

300
00:25:06.647 --> 00:25:09.081
Good news. bob.
You're staying for dinner.

301
00:25:09.116 --> 00:25:10.549
<i>I am?</i>

302
00:25:10.584 --> 00:25:13.919
One word of an american coming
to call and that was that.

303
00:25:16.022 --> 00:25:17.856
<i>This is like a thanksgiving.</i>

304
00:25:17.891 --> 00:25:19.591
Oh. what is that?

305
00:25:19.625 --> 00:25:22.293
It's a holiday roosevelt made of
the fourth Thursday of November.

306
00:25:22.328 --> 00:25:24.762
Everyone sits at the table.
eats turkey and argues.

307
00:25:24.796 --> 00:25:27.298
roosevelt is a great man.

308
00:25:27.332 --> 00:25:29.466
You eat as much as you like.
eh? Ella.

309
00:25:29.501 --> 00:25:31.502
The beans are from the garden.

310
00:25:31.536 --> 00:25:33.837
You didn't touch the feta.

311
00:25:33.872 --> 00:25:35.739
We save it for tomorrow. huh?

312
00:25:35.773 --> 00:25:38.141
Nay. mama. I'll start cleaning up.

313
00:25:38.176 --> 00:25:41.311
You see? Good girl. my stella.

314
00:25:41.345 --> 00:25:42.546
Good greek girl.

315
00:25:46.884 --> 00:25:48.684
You're on your own now.

316
00:25:48.719 --> 00:25:50.520
I think I can handle this one.

317
00:25:51.955 --> 00:25:53.689
So. mama.

318
00:25:53.724 --> 00:25:55.921
What's a greek girl like
you doing in melbourne?

319
00:25:55.956 --> 00:25:57.719
Oh. you don't care about that.

320
00:25:57.754 --> 00:26:00.053
No no no. don't go officer on me.
You're not australian.

321
00:26:00.088 --> 00:26:02.555
No no. I come from the old country.

322
00:26:02.589 --> 00:26:03.922
Smyrna.

323
00:26:03.956 --> 00:26:06.492
That was sacked by the turks.
wasn't it?

324
00:26:06.526 --> 00:26:08.395
You know about that?

325
00:26:08.429 --> 00:26:11.297
Um. I read a lot.

326
00:26:11.331 --> 00:26:14.832
<i>The turks invaded in 1922
and burned it down.</i>

327
00:26:14.866 --> 00:26:17.368
All gone.

328
00:26:17.402 --> 00:26:21.204
If you survived.
you fled like my mama and me.

329
00:26:21.238 --> 00:26:24.139
But we made it down to the docks.

330
00:26:24.173 --> 00:26:26.640
We swam to a ship.

331
00:26:26.674 --> 00:26:28.542
<i>The captain took us onboard</i>

332
00:26:28.577 --> 00:26:30.880
<i>And sailed us to piraeus.</i>

333
00:26:32.284 --> 00:26:34.019
He saved our lives.

334
00:26:34.053 --> 00:26:37.089
But our home was gone.

335
00:26:37.123 --> 00:26:39.624
So where do we go?

336
00:26:40.793 --> 00:26:42.460
We come here.

337
00:26:42.495 --> 00:26:45.864
We live. we work. we find love.

338
00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:50.201
So now you must tell
about your home.

339
00:26:50.236 --> 00:26:52.270
- Yeah. about america.
- No no no.

340
00:26:52.305 --> 00:26:54.874
Not america. America
we see in the movies.

341
00:26:54.908 --> 00:26:57.843
Your family. - Well.
seems like we both escaped disaster.

342
00:26:57.877 --> 00:26:59.311
I fled the leckie household.

343
00:26:59.345 --> 00:27:01.880
<i>How many in the household?</i>

344
00:27:01.914 --> 00:27:03.814
Mama had five girls. three boys.

345
00:27:03.849 --> 00:27:07.716
<i>Oh. okto babies. Your lucky mama.</i>

346
00:27:07.751 --> 00:27:11.186
I don't know if she'd agree.
She was almost 40 by the time I came along.

347
00:27:14.257 --> 00:27:15.957
She was worn out by then.

348
00:27:15.991 --> 00:27:17.992
I think they all were.

349
00:27:18.026 --> 00:27:21.662
But it's a blessing.
so many children.

350
00:27:21.696 --> 00:27:24.198
Well. marion and foddy leckie.
they didn't want eight kids.

351
00:27:24.232 --> 00:27:26.833
Oh no? They should stop?

352
00:27:26.867 --> 00:27:29.202
They didn't want the
beautiful baby son?

353
00:27:29.236 --> 00:27:32.971
I was last. Lasts least.

354
00:27:33.006 --> 00:27:35.574
<i>Oh. bob. You know nothing.</i>

355
00:27:36.576 --> 00:27:39.078
<i>By the time I met nicola</i>

356
00:27:39.112 --> 00:27:41.881
It was almost too
late to make babies.

357
00:27:41.915 --> 00:27:44.917
Not that we didn't try. eh?

358
00:27:46.185 --> 00:27:47.886
Eh. relax.

359
00:27:50.458 --> 00:27:53.230
That girl in the kitchen.
my only blessing.

360
00:27:53.264 --> 00:27:55.231
<i>I prayed for more.</i>

361
00:27:55.266 --> 00:27:57.400
But god said no.

362
00:27:57.435 --> 00:28:00.470
Nothing u can do when god say no.

363
00:28:02.206 --> 00:28:05.943
So where do you stay
with the marines. huh?

364
00:28:05.977 --> 00:28:08.145
They put us in the stadium
where you play cricket.

365
00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:11.316
What?  You will stay here tonight.

366
00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:14.750
Stella. get the bed ready
in the spare room. - Okay.

367
00:28:14.785 --> 00:28:16.784
I can't do that.

368
00:28:16.819 --> 00:28:18.586
I could never repay you.

369
00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:20.853
You can help clear the
vines from the roof.

370
00:28:20.887 --> 00:28:22.820
Yes. you will do that.

371
00:28:22.855 --> 00:28:26.389
You will make this your home.

372
00:28:32.695 --> 00:28:34.029
Twist a guy's arm.

373
00:30:26.244 --> 00:30:28.045
Oh. dear god.

374
00:30:31.349 --> 00:30:33.517
Oh. god.

375
00:30:35.854 --> 00:30:38.756
No no no. you stay right there.

376
00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:41.458
I think we should
give it another go.

377
00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:46.297
Okay. sure. Sure. me too.

378
00:30:47.266 --> 00:30:50.368
But even americans need. uh...

379
00:30:50.402 --> 00:30:53.804
Oh. a wait before they reload.

380
00:30:53.839 --> 00:30:56.007
It's okay.

381
00:30:57.242 --> 00:30:58.976
I'm going nowhere.

382
00:31:21.503 --> 00:31:23.971
Why did you have to
flee your family?

383
00:31:24.006 --> 00:31:26.541
Oh...

384
00:31:26.575 --> 00:31:29.143
My family.

385
00:31:30.912 --> 00:31:33.080
Not happy bunch. my family.

386
00:31:33.114 --> 00:31:35.015
Why not?

387
00:31:45.026 --> 00:31:47.394
<i>Something in our bones.</i>

388
00:31:49.097 --> 00:31:51.931
Did your brothers go into
the service to get away too?

389
00:31:51.966 --> 00:31:55.034
One of my brothers was too old.

390
00:31:55.068 --> 00:31:57.369
The other died when I was a kid.

391
00:31:57.404 --> 00:32:00.005
That's terrible.

392
00:32:00.039 --> 00:32:03.808
My father's been crippled
in the head ever since.

393
00:32:05.477 --> 00:32:07.612
We're not exactly the
judge hardy family.

394
00:32:09.381 --> 00:32:14.152
I swear. I'm just waiting for
the clock to hit midnight

395
00:32:14.186 --> 00:32:16.889
And my sanity bell goes ringing.

396
00:32:16.924 --> 00:32:20.393
I had a brother who died too.

397
00:32:20.428 --> 00:32:22.330
You did?

398
00:32:24.333 --> 00:32:26.168
No one talks about him.

399
00:32:26.202 --> 00:32:29.171
He was a baby. just a few
days old and didn't live.

400
00:32:29.205 --> 00:32:33.109
Mama got sick and wasn't
able to have any more.

401
00:33:46.008 --> 00:33:48.639
We start by restoring their bodies.

402
00:33:48.673 --> 00:33:51.374
They rest. They play.

403
00:33:51.408 --> 00:33:53.908
Then we work on their pride.

404
00:33:53.943 --> 00:33:55.943
We remind them they're marines.

405
00:33:55.978 --> 00:33:58.712
Then we replenish our supplies.

406
00:33:58.746 --> 00:34:00.980
See. all of that takes money.

407
00:34:01.014 --> 00:34:02.581
I understand.

408
00:34:02.615 --> 00:34:05.015
It's a hell of a lot
easier to raise money

409
00:34:05.049 --> 00:34:07.650
When there's a face behind it

410
00:34:07.684 --> 00:34:10.685
And a heroic story attached to it.

411
00:34:10.719 --> 00:34:12.319
Like yours.

412
00:34:12.354 --> 00:34:13.986
I don't feel like a hero. sir.

413
00:34:14.021 --> 00:34:16.087
You're not getting it.

414
00:34:16.121 --> 00:34:18.555
All right.

415
00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:21.591
Think of us out there on that line.

416
00:34:21.625 --> 00:34:23.492
You know what we need--

417
00:34:23.527 --> 00:34:26.754
New weapons. new gear.
better motor transport.

418
00:34:26.788 --> 00:34:28.186
Yes. sir.

419
00:34:28.221 --> 00:34:31.250
Selling u.S. War bonds

420
00:34:31.284 --> 00:34:35.980
Is just as important.
just as life saving.

421
00:34:36.014 --> 00:34:38.247
As what you did on guadalcanal.

422
00:34:38.281 --> 00:34:41.683
Now pack your seabag.
You're going home

423
00:34:41.717 --> 00:34:44.619
Home?

424
00:34:44.653 --> 00:34:47.521
How many u.S.
War bonds you gonna sell

425
00:34:47.555 --> 00:34:49.990
Stomping around the
solomons with us?

426
00:34:52.126 --> 00:34:54.927
Go on. Get it done. sergeant.

427
00:35:34.358 --> 00:35:35.926
Oh.

428
00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:37.961
I liked that.

429
00:35:37.996 --> 00:35:40.597
Shame on me.

430
00:35:42.466 --> 00:35:44.400
I got you something.

431
00:36:00.209 --> 00:36:02.477
Stockings?

432
00:36:02.511 --> 00:36:05.513
Real silk?

433
00:36:11.419 --> 00:36:13.954
I'll wear them only for you.

434
00:36:16.923 --> 00:36:18.657
Wait wait wait wait. There's more.

435
00:36:18.692 --> 00:36:20.959
This...

436
00:36:20.994 --> 00:36:23.027
Is for everyone.

437
00:36:23.062 --> 00:36:25.629
oh no.

438
00:36:25.664 --> 00:36:27.665
- Leg of lamb.
- No.

439
00:36:27.699 --> 00:36:29.900
How? How did you get it?

440
00:36:29.934 --> 00:36:32.035
<i>- I'm not gonna tell you.
- Why not?</i>

441
00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:34.471
I think you should
kiss me first. - How?

442
00:36:34.505 --> 00:36:36.206
- How?
- I can't remember.

443
00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:39.141
I can't get a leg of lamb.
But you do?

444
00:36:39.176 --> 00:36:42.378
American ingenuity and a
few packs of lucky strikes.

445
00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:46.181
"nicholas genkos-- wounded.

446
00:36:46.215 --> 00:36:48.716
Is that theodora genkos' nephew?

447
00:36:48.750 --> 00:36:51.984
<i>I don't know.</i>

448
00:36:52.018 --> 00:36:55.250
Baba checks the casualty
reports for greek names.

449
00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:00.955
"alexi stavrapolous."

450
00:37:00.989 --> 00:37:03.022
<i>What's happened to alexi?</i>

451
00:37:09.961 --> 00:37:11.328
"killed in action."

452
00:37:15.665 --> 00:37:17.999
I grew up with alexi.

453
00:37:18.034 --> 00:37:21.036
He lived two streets over.

454
00:37:23.739 --> 00:37:25.873
Change your shirt. nicola.

455
00:37:25.908 --> 00:37:28.576
We'll take them the spanikopita.

456
00:37:28.610 --> 00:37:31.446
I'd like to go.

457
00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:35.016
Pay my respects.
if they're friends of yours.

458
00:37:54.578 --> 00:37:57.079
- This is my friend bob.
- My condolences.

459
00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:02.819
<i>Stella and alexi.</i>

460
00:38:02.853 --> 00:38:05.322
They were friends every
since they were children.

461
00:38:05.356 --> 00:38:07.992
He was her friend.

462
00:38:48.192 --> 00:38:51.561
It's no good. bob.
Mama is still up.

463
00:39:20.726 --> 00:39:22.927
The greek boys are gone.

464
00:39:22.961 --> 00:39:25.563
<i>More and more every day--</i>

465
00:39:26.865 --> 00:39:30.234
<i>All of stella's friends from
when she grew up.</i>

466
00:39:30.268 --> 00:39:32.802
I prayed for them.

467
00:39:32.837 --> 00:39:35.271
And for their families.

468
00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.641
Do you go to church. bob?

469
00:39:40.943 --> 00:39:43.245
- St. Mary's.
- Mmm.

470
00:39:43.279 --> 00:39:45.147
What kind of church?

471
00:39:45.182 --> 00:39:47.116
Catholic.

472
00:39:49.219 --> 00:39:51.987
I like the early morning mass.

473
00:39:52.022 --> 00:39:53.389
Less people.

474
00:39:53.423 --> 00:39:55.960
<i>Hmm. we need prayers.</i>

475
00:39:57.263 --> 00:39:59.464
How are we going to make
it through these years

476
00:39:59.499 --> 00:40:01.733
If we don't pray?

477
00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:07.205
We are so lucky to
have you with us. bob.

478
00:40:07.239 --> 00:40:10.074
<i>To have a young man in the house.</i>

479
00:40:10.109 --> 00:40:12.243
<i>I like you.</i>

480
00:40:12.277 --> 00:40:15.680
Stella. she likes you.

481
00:40:15.714 --> 00:40:19.083
Baba. he always
wanted a son like you.

482
00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:23.789
I am going to pray that
you come back to us.

483
00:40:29.060 --> 00:40:32.529
That's a good thing to pray for.

484
00:41:02.127 --> 00:41:04.161
<i>Let's get your squads going!</i>

485
00:41:04.196 --> 00:41:07.298
<i>Pack up that gear anlet's go!</i>

486
00:41:09.234 --> 00:41:12.303
What?  You thought uncle sam was gonna
pay for your great debauch forever?

487
00:41:12.337 --> 00:41:14.805
<i>- Officer: Move out!
- Semper fi.</i>

488
00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:18.009
Better gear up. brother.

489
00:41:19.144 --> 00:41:21.579
Time to get back in shape.

490
00:41:33.625 --> 00:41:35.225
<i>The m1 is better.
Thers no contest.</i>

491
00:41:35.260 --> 00:41:37.161
<i>- Runner: Okay. so show us.
- Okay. ready ready.</i>

492
00:41:37.195 --> 00:41:39.262
oh!

493
00:41:39.297 --> 00:41:41.064
Eight rounds over five.

494
00:41:41.099 --> 00:41:43.266
No. I will still
keep my springfield.

495
00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:45.635
<i>Jesus christ. it's like
talking to an old lady.</i>

496
00:41:45.669 --> 00:41:47.136
Fine. You know what?
Keep your cane.

497
00:41:47.171 --> 00:41:48.538
- I will keep my cane.
- I'll will take the m1

498
00:41:48.572 --> 00:41:50.273
Because it beats the hell
out of the springfield.

499
00:41:50.307 --> 00:41:52.575
<i>No no no no. The 03's are
still more accurate.</i>

500
00:41:52.609 --> 00:41:54.777
<i>- Exactly exactly.
- No no. they're slower.</i>

501
00:41:54.812 --> 00:41:55.979
- Target in sight.
- Watch him miss.

502
00:41:56.013 --> 00:41:58.048
<i>- Do it!
- Come on. gibson.</i>

503
00:41:58.082 --> 00:42:00.284
Come on. gibson.

504
00:42:00.318 --> 00:42:01.918
bull's-eye!

